# Booth & speaker recommender — Patch the Conference hackathon

Event: https://luma.com/ptc — Bright Data, 625 2nd St SF, 9am–7pm, demo on stage 5:30.
Tracks: FalkorDB (iPhone Duos) · Bright Data ($1.5k) · TrueForge/TrueFoundry + OpenAI ($1k) · Qodo ($500)

## Original sketch
truefoundary with jev style queries. The user discusses what they care about;
this leads to a classifier with truefoundry, and brightdata agents go out until
the confidence score is hit. Goal: know who, the priority, and what to talk
about — not just a list of the who.

## One sentence
"Tell it what you're here for. It tells you which booths to visit and which
speakers to meet, in order, and exactly why. When it's not sure, it figures
out how to find out."

Pitch: recommender systems have never told you why. This one does, cites its
sources, and goes and checks when it isn't confident.

## What the user sees
  Me: I'm building a RAG app, our scraping keeps breaking, and I want to cut LLM costs.

  1. Bright Data — booth 4
     Because: your scraping keeps breaking → Web Unlocker handles blocked sites  [source]
     Ask them: "Does the MCP server work with my agent framework?"

  2. Talk to <speaker> — 2:30 talk, also at FalkorDB booth
     Because: you're building RAG → they built FalkorDB's GraphRAG SDK  [source: GitHub]
     Ask them: "How does GraphRAG compare on cost vs vector RAG?"

  3. FalkorDB — booth 2
     Because: RAG → GraphRAG is their headline use case  [source: docs]
     ⚠️ not sure they help with cost → reading their pricing page… ✅ 0.91

  Skip: Qodo — code review isn't on your list.

Then: "actually cost matters most" → list reorders, reasons change, new gap → new fetch.

## Entities
- Sponsors / exhibitors: company, products, claims, pricing, booth.
- Speakers, two layers:
  - Trivial: what they're talking about — title, time, abstract from the agenda.
  - Interesting: WHO they are — role, company, what they've built (GitHub repos,
    papers, products), past talks, what they post about, whether they're at a booth.
    That's how we get "talk to X because they built Y, ask them Z".
  - Public professional info only (speakers/sponsor reps are public in this context).
    No personal/contact scraping.

## Three decisions, one fast model (System One: clef / nimble on Ollama)
The LLM writes the rules and the sentences; the decision model applies them, many times, quickly.

1. SCORE — does this booth/speaker match a criterion?  (Noul / Score per criterion)
2. GAP   — am I sure enough?  (calibrated probability vs threshold)
3. STRATEGY — what's the cheapest way to find out?  (Choice over Bright Data strategies)

Loop: score → unsure? → pick strategy → fetch → write evidence → rescore → escalate if still unsure.
All three are the same `decide(context, question, options)` call. Strategies already
tried drop out of the options, so escalation comes for free. Log every decision + probability
→ visible trace ("picked Unlocker 0.81 over SERP 0.12").

Model choice: clef (best calibrated, ECE 0.040) for gap/confidence; Nimble v4 Max (66.6 index)
where accuracy matters more. Avoid Nimble 9B v3 for confidence (ECE 0.12).

## Criteria document (written live by the TrueForge agent as the user talks)
  {
    "criteria": [
      {"id":"scraping","type":"noul","q":"Helps with scraping that breaks/gets blocked","weight":0.4},
      {"id":"rag","type":"noul","q":"Relevant to building a RAG app","weight":0.3},
      {"id":"cost","type":"score","q":"Helps reduce LLM cost","scale":5,"weight":0.3},
      {"id":"recruiter","type":"noul","q":"Is purely a recruiting booth","veto":true}
    ],
    "threshold": 0.8
  }
User edits by talking → agent rewrites doc → everything rescores (cheap, fast).

## Bright Data strategy menu (the options for decision #3)
| Gap example                                   | Strategy                                   | Cost  |
|-----------------------------------------------|--------------------------------------------|-------|
| "does X help with cost?" (no URL yet)         | SERP API (search_engine / batch)           | cheap |
| "free tier?" (know the page)                  | Web Unlocker (scrape_as_markdown /pricing) | cheap |
| structured facts from a page                  | extract                                    | med   |
| company size / stage                          | Business Search — Ludicrous                | cheap |
| who is this speaker / who works at the booth  | Business Search People / LinkedIn scraper  | med   |
| what has this speaker built                   | GitHub scraper / SERP → Unlocker           | med   |
| do real users like it                         | Web Scraper API (G2, Reddit)               | med   |
| where's the booth / interactive agenda        | Browser API                                | slow  |
| hard entity match, needs ranking              | Business Search — Smart                    | $$    |
Escalation ladder: SERP → Unlocker → extract/Scraper → Smart Business Search → Browser.
Pitch to Bright Data: agents shouldn't have to know your catalog; a decision model routes to
the right product and only escalates when the cheap one wasn't enough.

## FalkorDB = agent memory; the reason IS a graph path
  (You)-[CARES_ABOUT]->(flaky scraping)<-[SOLVES]-(Web Unlocker)<-[SELLS]-(Bright Data)-[AT]->(Booth 4)
                                                       └-[EVIDENCE {p:0.94, src:url}]
  (You)-[CARES_ABOUT]->(RAG)<-[BUILT]-(Speaker)-[WORKS_AT]->(FalkorDB)-[AT]->(Booth 2)
                                         └-[GIVES]->(Talk @2:30)

Nodes: User, Criterion, Topic, Sponsor, Product, Speaker, Talk, Project, Booth, Source, GapType, Strategy
Three kinds of memory:
- What you care about: Criterion nodes + weights, edited live.
- What we know: claims/evidence edges with decision-model probability + source. Re-used; no re-scrape.
- What worked: (GapType)-[TRIED]->(Strategy) {conf_before, conf_after}. Strategy picker learns
  "pricing questions → Unlocker /pricing works, skip SERP". Agent gets cheaper over the session.
Recommendation = top-ranked paths; sentence = path read out loud. ~4 hops = their benchmark case.
Stretch: cross-attendee memory ("12 people who cared about cost went to FalkorDB first").

## Stack
- TrueForge (TrueFoundry) + OpenAI: conversation → criteria doc; paths → sentences; agent runtime.
  Pitch: "decision node" as a drop-in TrueForge skill (verify they don't already have one).
- clef / nimble via Ollama (Pydantic AI `system-one` models): score, gap, strategy.
- Bright Data (MCP / APIs): evidence gathering.
- FalkorDB: memory + explainable paths (show highlighted path in FalkorDB browser on stage).
- Qodo: run our PRs through it.
- UI: one web page, three panes — chat | live criteria | ranked booths & speakers with reasons + "checking…" trace.

## Demo script (~3 min)
1. "Here's what I'm here for" → criteria build themselves on screen.
2. Ranked booths + speakers appear with "Because:" lines and sources; a couple show ⚠️ unsure.
3. Agents fetch ("reading their pricing page…"); unsure items lock in.
4. "Actually cost matters most" → reshuffle, new reasons, new gap → new fetch.
5. 20s for judges: the reason is a FalkorDB path; strategy choice is a decision model; drop-in for TrueForge.
Run it on THIS event's sponsors and speakers.

